course: dictionary
lesson: 10387
title: Trench: a ditch, a war, or a deep valley in the sea
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the words around it tell you which meaning is meant. «The workers dug a trench for the new water pipes» shows a trench is a long, narrow ditch in the ground, because of the digging and the pipes.
explain: «Soldiers lived in muddy trenches in the First World War» is a different meaning. The clues are soldiers, war and mud, so a trench here is a ditch that soldiers dug to protect themselves. Check by swapping your meaning into the sentence: if it still makes sense, you have chosen well.
explain: A trench coat is a long, belted raincoat. The word trench here does not mean a ditch at all, so always read the whole phrase before you decide what a word means.
fact: A trench is a long, narrow ditch dug in the ground.
fact: In war, soldiers dug trenches to protect themselves from enemy fire, especially in the First World War.
fact: An ocean trench is a very deep, long valley in the sea floor.
fact: «The deepest known point of the ocean is the Challenger Deep in the Mariana Trench in the Pacific, about 10,900 metres down.»
fact: A trench coat is a long, belted raincoat.
exercise: «At Christmas 1914 soldiers on both sides stopped fighting in no man's land, the ground between the trenches.» In this sentence, what are trenches? | Deep valleys in the sea floor | *Long ditches that soldiers dug to live in and protect themselves | Tall hills covered in snow | Long, belted raincoats
exercise: «The Mariana Trench is about 11 km deep, deeper than Mount Everest is tall.» In this sentence, what is a trench? | A long, belted raincoat | A ditch for pipes in a garden | *A very deep, long valley in the sea floor | A railway tunnel through mountains
exercise: Mum says, «Wear your trench coat, it is pouring with rain.» What is a trench coat? | A tent for camping | *A long, belted raincoat | A ditch you can wear | A warm woolly hat
test: «The Challenger Deep in the Mariana Trench is the deepest point of the ocean.» What does trench mean in this sentence? | A tent for soldiers | *A very deep, long valley in the sea floor | A belted raincoat | A long, narrow ditch dug for pipes
test: «One plate diving under another makes trenches and volcanoes.» What does trenches mean here? | Long, belted raincoats | *Deep valleys in the sea floor where plates meet | A tall, snowy mountain range | Muddy ditches for soldiers in war
exam: Which sentence uses trench to mean a long, narrow ditch dug in the ground? | *The plumber dug a trench for the new water pipes. | She wore her trench coat to school in the rain. | The Mariana Trench is deeper than Mount Everest is tall. | The soldiers were tired after the long march.
